<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oranmiyan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oranmiyan is a legendary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> prince, described in oral tradition as the youngest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oduduwa">Oduduwa</a>. Tradition credits him with founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">savannah</a> north of the forest zone. He is also associated in some traditions with the origins of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oranmiyan.jpg" alt="Oranmiyan" width="272" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abby's Lens · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Opa_Oranmiyan_at_Ile-Ife_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is known of Oranmiyan's childhood, and most of what is told comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a> sources. Most name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oduduwa">Oduduwa</a> as his father, but he is also called a man of two fathers, Oduduwa and Ogun, who are both said to have had relations with his mother, Lakange Anihunka, a slave captured by Ogun in war; some oral versions give her Yagba or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nupe-kingdom">Nupe</a> heritage. The legend says he was half light-skinned like Ogun and half dark like Oduduwa, which may suggest vitiligo, and that this gave him his name, read as &quot;the child has chosen to be controversial&quot;.</p><p>His other name, Odede, marks him as a great hunter. He was also a warrior, the first Odole Oduduwa, and since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a> had no standing army he took up its defence as the first Akogun of Ife.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Tradition holds that the Ooni of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a> sent him to Igodomigodo when a crisis among its administrators left it without a ruler. He camped at a place called Use and governed from there, but his foreign style of rule displeased some chiefs, who spied on him. Declaring that only a son of the soil could manage the people, he named the land Ile-Ibinu, &quot;Land of Vexation&quot;, later corrupted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>. At Egor he married Erinmwide, daughter of the Enogie of Egor, and their son Eweka went on to establish the Benin monarchy, whose dynasty still rules.</p><p>Moving north with his followers, he settled near the river Moshi, a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>, and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-ile">Oyo-Ile</a>, which his descendants expanded into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a>; early accounts date this to 1300. He fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bariba">Bariba</a>, his northern neighbours, and married Torosi, a Tapa princess who became the mother of Sango, as well as Moremi Ajasoro.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>His family is said to have raised the Staff of Oranmiyan, Opa Oranmiyan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a>, after his death. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">obelisk</a> stands 5.5 metres tall in a grove in Mopa, Ile-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a>; a storm in 1884 broke about 1.2 metres from its top, and it has twice fallen and been set up again. Radiocarbon tests have shown that it was raised centuries before the start of the Oduduwa dynasty. In 2012 his descendant Oba Lamidi Adeyemi III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo</a> began the annual Oranyan Festival, held between 8 and 15 September.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ọranyan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Oranmiyan</b>, Oduduwa's last-born and one of his father's principal ministers, was sent to govern the nascent Edo kingdom when its people asked for Oduduwa's rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ife">Kingdom of Ife · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Yoruba oral tradition attributes its founding to <b>Oranmiyan</b>, remembered as a son or grandson of Oduduwa, the mythical progenitor of the Yoruba people.</blockquote><a href="/empire/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The last Ogiso, Owodo, was banished, and after an interregnum under the administrators Evian and Ogiamien the Edo chiefs invited Prince <b>Oranmiyan</b> of the Kingdom of Ife.</blockquote><a href="/state/igodomigodo-ogiso-dynasty">Igodomigodo (Ogiso dynasty)</a></li></ul></section>
